# rules: swift-webview-file-access-from-urls
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-200-webview.swift
# from: CWE-200 (Exposure of Sensitive Information), CWE-749, MASVS-PLATFORM-2
#
# `allowFileAccessFromFileURLs` lets a document loaded from a `file:` URL fetch
# other `file:` URLs, and `allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs` drops the
# same-origin policy for such a document altogether. Either one turns a single
# line of injected script in a local page into a read of the app's whole
# container and a POST of it anywhere.
#
# Both are undocumented WebKit preferences reached by string through
# `setValue(_:forKey:)`, which is the tell: there is no typed API for them,
# so they arrive in a codebase copied from an answer on the internet rather
# than chosen. `loadFileURL(_:allowingReadAccessTo:)` is the supported way to
# give a local page access to exactly the directory it needs, and it is
# narrower than either flag by construction.
#
# The key is what the rule reads and the value decides whether it reports, so
# `setValue(false, forKey:)` - which is somebody explicitly turning it off, and
# not rare, because the default changed - is left alone.
#
# The receiver is a hole because both `config.preferences` and `config` are
# real spellings of the same mistake, and it is variadic because a single hole
# reaches only a bare name. See GUIDE.md.

[ "$$$C.setValue($V, forKey: $K)", "$$$C.setValue($V, forKey: $K, $$$_)" ] as $prefs
| "^\"allow(?:File|Universal)Access(?:From)?FileURLs\"$" as $isFileAccess
| "^true$" as $isOn
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $prefs) as $all
| ( $all | of($prefs)
    | where_capture("K"; $isFileAccess)
    | where_capture("V"; $isOn)
    | focus("K") )
| finding("swift-webview-file-access-from-urls";
    "$K lets a local page in this web view read the app's other files and "
    + "send them out; loadFileURL(_:allowingReadAccessTo:) grants one "
    + "directory instead")
| report
